rows=[(553, 'nothing-is-ruined', 'Nothing is ruined', 'The poem turns domestic self-examination into permission to live: milk has reduced into khoa and a spicy dish need not become a crisis. The ordinary saucepan is a trace of forgiving oneself, without staging an immaculate kitchen or shaming household work.', 'Horizontal 3:2 loose gouache and colored-pencil still life of a small stainless-steel saucepan off the heat, resting on one folded cotton pad on a modest Indian kitchen counter. Oblique overhead view, pot in lower left with one short dark handle extending left, plausible attachment and geometry. A shallow amount of softly grainy off-white reduced milk solids remains inside, slightly cream at the edges, clearly food rather than foam. One plain steel spoon has its bowl inside and handle supported on the rim. Dull soft metal reflections, incomplete pencil contours and warm cream/cool grey paint planes, quiet empty counter to right. Modest morning window light, matter-of-fact and gently humorous. No flame, smoking burnt mess, elaborate dessert, luxury appliances, arranged ingredients, teacup, flowers, recipe text, people or moral symbols. Selective detail, not metallic CGI polish.'), (558, 'after-the-tractor', 'After the tractor', 'A scarecrow fears replacement and is crushed by mechanisation. Show its discarded material presence at a worked field edge, preserving its vulnerable voice without comic mascot treatment or claiming to document a particular temple-owned field.', 'Horizontal 3:2 expressive charcoal and earth-toned watercolor, low viewpoint at the edge of a recently worked Indian field. In lower left lies a collapsed handmade straw scarecrow: a short wooden cross-frame with worn plain cotton draped over it, loosened straw bundle arms and one small ordinary round clay-pot head resting on its side beside the frame. Pot has just two faded dark eye marks and one short mouth line, visibly a farm object, never a human skull, real portrait or smiling cartoon. Straw and cloth snag and settle naturally against soil with no floating attachments. Across middle ground two broad fresh tractor-tire impressions continue diagonally away and dissolve into a pale open field. No tractor required. Right and upper half quiet muted dry-earth washes with subdued green at distant boundary. Used but not grotesquely shredded, no blood, bones, deity, bull, shrine, machine logos or historical location claims. Sparse charcoal at material contacts and broad breathable wash: dignity of something displaced, no apocalyptic ruin.'), (574, 'beyond-our-reach', 'Beyond our reach', 'Love is found under a Southern Magnolia after tulips are gone, with flowers beyond human reach. An upward view of a high branch preserves that intimate shift of attention without inventing the author and companion or collaging later metaphors.', 'Horizontal 3:2 botanical watercolor and graphite looking gently upward at a high Southern magnolia branch entering from upper left. Two large creamy-white Magnolia grandiflora blossoms at different stages: one open with broad overlapping cup-like petals and pale central cone of stamens, the other partly opening. A few thick glossy dark-green elliptical leaves, some with warm rusty-brown undersides. Coherent branching and real short flower stalks, irregular leaf groups rather than repeated decoration. Lower right and centre mostly pale warm sky with a faint dissolving wash, suggesting unreachable height and space for breath. Soft summer light across petals, subdued leaf reflections, selective graphite near branch joints. No tulips, chinar leaves, butterflies as extra symbols, people, ornate gates, text or flower-catalogue staging. Tender botanical paint, not every leaf equally sharp; opaque ivory paper.')]
